   "author": "Hollowmere team",
   "story": "The Pruetts lived in a log cabin in the firs, and Dale had been promising his daughter Josie a treehouse since she was eight. When the Bloom came and the Cordon shut the valley he built three in nine days: plank huts on decks round three big firs, joined by rope bridges his brother Curtis strung, reached only by ladders they pulled up every night. The Hollowed can't climb. They stood at the foot of the trees and waited, and when three of them got into the cabin and wouldn't leave, Dale burned it with them inside. The family lived on the garden, the rain barrels and the pulley lift all summer. Then the garden went to frost. Curtis went down alone for the potatoes in the old root cellar and came back bitten and said nothing; the Bloom went through the bunk hut, then the kitchen. Dale sent Josie off down the back ladder toward the river, had her padlock him into the highest hut with the stores, and waited. They were right about the climbing. It was hunger that brought them down.",
